newly discovered bugs get appended here as they're found -- not fixed
inline unless that's the task at hand (see CLAUDE.md, "track newly
discovered bugs"). one entry per bug, appended in the order found,
each shaped like:

    - <canonical-name> (<file>:<line>): <short plain-text description
      of what's broken and how it was confirmed, no back-quotes>

        <js code that triggers it, or a one-line note if not
        practically reproducible in plain js>

canonical-name is a short kebab-case handle for referring back to the
bug elsewhere (commit messages, other BUGS entries). the source
location is the primary place the bug lives, not every place it's
felt. keep the description to a sentence or two -- just enough to
re-find and re-confirm the bug without re-deriving it.

- option-key-casing-silently-ignored (lws-context.c:110,
  client_connect_info_fromobj): option parsers are split between
  camelcase (createContext()'s info, vhostName, sslCa, ...) and
  snake_case (client_connect_info_fromobj: local_protocol_name,
  ssl_connection, local_port, keep_warm_secs, auth_username,
  auth_password) -- a wrong-cased key is silently ignored, not
  rejected. caused a real, multi-hour-to-diagnose hang: connection
  opens and negotiates, then sits there because no protocol object
  ever got bound. found via localProtocolName vs local_protocol_name
  in HttpClientProtocol.connect() (lib/lws/protocols.js).

    import { LWSContext } from 'lws';

    const ctx = new LWSContext({
      protocols: [{ name: 'chat', onClientEstablished(wsi) {} }],
    });

    ctx.clientConnect({
      address: 'example.com', port: 443, path: '/', host: 'example.com',
      ssl_connection: 1,
      localProtocolName: 'chat', // wrong casing, silently dropped
    });
    // handshake completes but onClientEstablished never fires --
    // correct key is local_protocol_name (snake_case)

- check-access-rights-oob-buffer (lws-protocol.c:572,
  lwsjs_callback_protocol): LWS_CALLBACK_CHECK_ACCESS_RIGHTS is
  handled with the same code path as ADD_HEADERS/PROCESS_HTML, which
  treats args.max_len as a buffer capacity. confirmed against
  libwebsockets' server.c: for CHECK_ACCESS_RIGHTS, lws actually sets
  args.max_len to the mount's auth_mask bitmask, unrelated to any
  buffer size, while args.p/args.len point into the small
  header-parse buffer. the memset and the ArrayBuffer handed to js
  are both sized off that bitmask, so both go out of bounds whenever
  the bitmask exceeds the real uri length.

    import { createServer } from 'lws';

    createServer({
      port: 9001,
      mounts: [{ mountpoint: '/', origin: '.', originProtocol: 'file',
                 authentication_mode: 1 }], // any nonzero auth_mask bit
      protocols: [{ name: 'http', onCheckAccessRights(wsi, buf) {
        // buf is sized/zeroed using the auth_mask bitmask, not the
        // real header-buffer length -- oob memset already happened
      } }],
    });

- ws-close-payload-oob-read (lws-protocol.c:555,
  lwsjs_callback_protocol): the synthesized ws-close handling inside
  the CLIENT_RECEIVE branch reads in[0]/in[1] as the close code with
  no check that len >= 2. the genuine WS_PEER_INITIATED_CLOSE handler
  a few lines below (lws-protocol.c:625) correctly guards the same
  read with len >= 2. per rfc 6455 a close frame legally carries 0 or
  1 payload bytes, so this path reads past the frame payload whenever
  a peer sends a bare or 1-byte close frame.

    // requires a peer that sends a ws close frame with 0-1 bytes of
    // payload to a client connection, so CLIENT_RECEIVE synthesizes
    // the close-frame path instead of WS_PEER_INITIATED_CLOSE
    // handling it directly
    import { LWSContext } from 'lws';
    const ctx = new LWSContext({ port: -1 });
    ctx.clientConnect({
      address: 'example.com', port: 443, path: '/', ssl_connection: 1,
      protocol: 'chat',
    });

- raw-client-immediate-close-fires-error-not-close (lib/lws/protocols.js:485,
  RawProtocol.onClientConnectionError): closing a raw TCP client wsi
  (TCPSocket/TCPSocketStream) synchronously from within its own 'open'
  handler - i.e. right after LWS_CALLBACK_RAW_CONNECTED fires - never
  triggers onRawClose ('close'); instead onClientConnectionError
  ('error', with an empty message) fires once and that's the last
  event the wsi ever produces. confirmed with an evented TCPSocket:
  the 'open' listener logs then calls close(), an 'error' listener
  logs "error: " (empty message), and 'close' never fires, even
  waiting several seconds. looks like a native lws timing/state-race
  between the RAW_CONNECTED callback and the wsi's internal
  established-state transition, not a JS-level issue - a caller
  waiting on 'close' (or a *Stream's `closed` promise, whose
  StreamAdapter#error only rejects `opened` and never resolves
  `closed`, see protocols.js's StreamAdapter doc) hangs forever if it
  closes this early instead of waiting for 'open' to fully settle
  first.

    import { TCPSocket } from './lib/tcpsocket.js';
    const s = new TCPSocket('127.0.0.1', <any raw TCP listener port>);
    s.addEventListener('open', () => s.close());
    s.addEventListener('error', e => console.log('error:', JSON.stringify(e.message)));
    s.addEventListener('close', () => console.log('close')); // never fires
